ld so if you look at the  bottom one it really is just fold on  list but it generalizes to other data  types this is something that's entirely  mechanical that you can generate from  the definition of a data type but  traditionally it's sort of obnoxious and  you have to write it yourself  theoretically you could do that with a  macro however not quite there yet  there's a few tricky details that if you  interested in hearing what they are talk  to me after the talk  another example maybe even more  ridiculous is maybe we could create a  macro that allows you to query from a  table using sequel and it uses the  expected type of what it's supposed to  return to bind variables that correspond  to the column names in the table maybe  people think that's ridiculous in a  horrible idea but I think it would be  cool if you could implement something  like this just to see if it worked  so anyway that was a lot of information  that I threw at you all at once but to  recap to summarize Hackett is a Haskell  extended with macros and this gives us  nice things it lets us extend the  language without having to modify the  compiler it lets us eliminate  boilerplate it lets us build nice DSL  with custom syntax macros allow us to  augment the metaphor facilities already  provided by Haskell it does not replace  them they work together in in a nice way  and type directed Macker are  theoretically available the interface  needs to be a lot better than it  currently is and there's still a lot of  work to do there but it's something that  because of the the inner leaf type  checking and macro expansion is  something that's possible in hack it  where's it's just completely out of the  question in most implementations of this  sort of thing and that's it any  questions  all right I am looking at slide Oh at  the moment and there's one question  there right now which is how does the  presence of Macker affect decidability  of the type system well so it's it's  very much because type checking and  macro system or macro expansion are one  of the same and macro expansion is  already undecidable you can write  arbitrary functions the type system is  obviously undecidable as well the type  system itself I guess isn't necessarily  undecidable it depends on how you kind  of define it  I think that's there's a lot of work  that needs to be done to sort of  theorize come up with a model for what  some of these things mean what does it  mean to have a macro system conjoined at  the hip with a type system it's not  something that has been extensively  researched but it's something that I'd  be interested in doing haven't done that  yet so maybe not a super satisfactory  answer but that's all I all I can say  all right so one other question  what does Hackett get us the template  Haskell doesn't that's a good question I  anticipated that it would be asked so I  actually have a couple of slides so this  is a question that I've been asked a  number of different times before but I  haven't really had a great explanation  for it in the past this time I do so the  basic difference between these two  things is the idea of macros versus  splices so some people would say the  template Haskell is a macro system I  would disagree but it is ultimately kind  of a fuzzy definition but what I think  of as a macro is something that  transforms code to code it has this type  that I talked about earlier template  Haskell doesn't have this it has splices  which take which are functions that  produce code and they are run at compile  time but they don't take code as input  why it does this matter well if you look  at the application of a macro like this  def server thing it's all able to have  any custom syntax at once a splice is  just an ordinary function that runs at  compile time so it has to still conform  to the syntax of Haskell so if you  wanted to encode that same thing on the  top in template Haskell syntax you would  have to write something really explicit  on the bottom which works okay in this  really simple example but when you have  really complex macros with optional  pieces to them and fancy grammars it you  really can't encode the things that you  would do with macros using a splice  but that's not all it turns out that  this type signature is actually a little  bit of a lie because in template Haskell  that ast that gets returned can't also  contain splices which means that you  have a really limited system where there  can only essentially be one layer of  splice transformation and then  everything has to stop that's really  limiting in terms of what you can do but  that's not all either it turns out that  GHC has a staged restriction which  basically says that if you define  something in the same module and then  try to use it at compile time you can't  you have to be in different modules  sometimes this is awkward if you end up  with kind of cyclical dependencies and  it's awkward to break them up into two  modules also you can't define a function  in or define a splice in the same module  where you use it which might not sound  like a big deal but then when you end up  doing really fancy Macker where you have  Macker that define macros and then you  use them it sounds ridiculous I know but  it is actually useful that doesn't work  with this kind of system but that's not  all either it turns out that first of  all Hacket handles that it turns out  that template haskell also imposes  various constraints on expansion order  so basically what happens is when you  have two splices at the top level of a  program  imagine make some data type is some  function that basically defines a data  type in terms of that has a field of the  second argument here so we're defining a  data type called foo that holds a bar  and a datatype called bar that holds a  foo normally that would be totally  reasonable to do in house call  doesn't work in template haskell because  the way template haskell works is it  basically just takes all of the splices  it chops up your file into chunks and  then it type checks them individually  and this causes problems because if you  have two things that refer to each other  it doesn't work and if you end up having  to you know have a situation where you  want to define something that's mutually  recursive you're kind of stuck hackett  handles that fine so there's a long list  of differences between these two things  and they really are designed to do  totally different things template a  school is not bad it's just designed to  do something simple simpler than what  hackett is designed to do it's very  useful i think writing tasks or writing  high school without template high school  would probably be kind of painful but  it's definitely it's a different thing  all right let's see other questions list  macros are easy in parts because the ast  is nested lists what's it like  manipulating the ast of Hackett's well  Hackett has list fee syntax for a reason  and indeed if you say look at the  definition of the expression in Haskell  this is the actual data type that  doesn't fit on the slide in fact it's  really big  this is just four expressions by the way  this is not the whole syntax tree this  is really hard to manipulate with macros  for precisely that reason it's it's very  difficult to predict what a particular  sequence of characters will translate  into in the ast and that makes it hard  to write extensible syntax with Hacket  just like in any other list but just  built out of lists identifiers and  strings and it's not it's not any more  complex to manipulate than it would be  in a traditional Lisp hack it does have  sort of in fixed syntax but it's  designed in such a way that that just D  sugars down into the the prefix syntax  and it's always explicit so it's not  difficult to parse it's a good question  I still have do I yes I still have some  time because I intentionally left a lot  of time all right so let's see what  would it take to use Hackett at phase  one that's a very racket specific  question if you're not familiar with the  racket two rackets phase one essentially  means that compile time so what would it  take to use Hackett's to write macros is  essentially the translation of that  question I think it's okay it's a good  question so theoretically not nothing  nothing prevents you from writing your  macros in Hacket itself so for a little  bit of context the macker that i've been  defining in hackett i've been right  using rackets to write the macros that  then expand into hackett code so it's a  dynamically typed macro definition  language that is mostly just because I  haven't got gotten around to  implementing like the the bindings the  the types for what it means to have  typed syntax transformers there's no  like fundamental reasons that can't be  the case it's just that I haven't gotten  around to implementing it and because  racket has a lot of really really nice  pattern matching facilities for handling  syntax and creating macros that would be  a non-trivial amount of work or maybe  not any new research per se to implement  in Hacket so there's nothing  theoretically that prevents it it would  just be some programming let's see a few  more questions  so macros can generate  Russians can they generate types  patterns yes and yes those are both  things that hackett supports is the  compiler slash runtime new from the  ground up or are you re using parts of  existing compilers for each language  it's a good question the way that racket  works is it's designed for essentially  adding new languages ghe is not so I'm  reusing a lot of racket I'm reusing the  racket macro system I didn't  reimplemented have to re-implement all  of the the GHC type system that's  unfortunately too different to really  reuse and again it's not really designed  for that sort of thing racket is so I'm  we're using a lot of the runtime support  from racket I'm reusing the macro system  and reusing the their pattern matching  system right now I'm reusing the whole  VM and runtime that they use but all of  the the sort of type checking stuff is  all new and built on top of that so  that's a lot of questions there's a few  more I think I'm going to let people  come and talk to me after the session  about those two those things instead of  forcing everybody else to listen to them  but by all means do so is still a bit of  time before the closing keynote and  thank you very much  [Applause]
 

